    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Let's start by understanding the problem. We are given that when an integer \\( x \\) is divided by 15, the remainder is 7. This can be expressed mathematically as:\n\\[ x = 15k + 7 \\]\nwhere \\( k \\) is an integer. Now, we need to find the remainders when \\( x \\) is divided by 3 and by 5.\n\nFirst, let's find the remainder when \\( x \\) is divided by 3. We substitute \\( x = 15k + 7 \\) into the expression for the remainder when divided by 3:\n\\[ x \\mod 3 = (15k + 7) \\mod 3 \\]\nSince \\( 15k \\equiv 0 \\pmod{3} \\) (because 15 is a multiple of 3), we have:\n\\[ x \\mod 3 = 7 \\mod 3 \\]\nNow, we calculate \\( 7 \\mod 3 \\):\n\\[ 7 \\div 3 = 2 \\text{ remainder } 1 \\]\nSo,\n\\[ 7 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3} \\]\nTherefore,\n\\[ x \\mod 3 = 1 \\]\n\nNext, let's find the remainder when \\( x \\) is divided by 5. We substitute \\( x = 15k + 7 \\) into the expression for the remainder when divided by 5:\n\\[ x \\mod 5 = (15k + 7) \\mod 5 \\]\nSince \\( 15k \\equiv 0 \\pmod{5} \\) (because 15 is a multiple of 5), we have:\n\\[ x \\mod 5 = 7 \\mod 5 \\]\nNow, we calculate \\( 7 \\mod 5 \\):\n\\[ 7 \\div 5 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 2 \\]\nSo,\n\\[ 7 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\nTherefore,\n\\[ x \\mod 5 = 2 \\]\n\nThe remainders when \\( x \\) is divided by 3 and by 5 are 1 and 2, respectively. The sum of these remainders is:\n\\[ 1 + 2 = 3 \\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{3} \\]",
