      "text": "To solve the problem, we start by expressing the given information in a mathematical form. We are told that when an integer \\( x \\) is divided by 15, the remainder is 7. This can be written as:\n\\[ x \\equiv 7 \\pmod{15} \\]\nThis means that \\( x \\) can be expressed in the form:\n\\[ x = 15k + 7 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\).\n\nNext, we need to find the remainders when \\( x \\) is divided by 3 and by 5. We will do this by considering different values of \\( k \\).\n\nFirst, let's find the remainder when \\( x \\) is divided by 3. We substitute \\( x = 15k + 7 \\) into the expression for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x \\equiv 7 \\pmod{3} \\]\nSince \\( 7 \\div 3 \\) has a remainder of 1, we have:\n\\[ 7 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3} \\]\nThus, the remainder when \\( x \\) is divided by 3 is 1.\n\nNext, let's find the remainder when \\( x \\) is divided by 5. We substitute \\( x = 15k + 7 \\) into the expression for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x \\equiv 7 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSince \\( 7 \\div 5 \\) has a remainder of 2, we have:\n\\[ 7 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\nThus, the remainder when \\( x \\) is divided by 5 is 2.\n\nNow, we need to find the sum of these remainders:\n\\[ 1 + 2 = 3 \\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the remainders when the same integer is divided by 3 and by 5 is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).",
